Line 189:
  BEFORE: The latest in anti-realist technology, Allan Gibbard's 'factual-normative worlds' are abstract objects designed to help explain the meanings both of simple evaluative statements and of complex sentences with evaluative components. A factual-normative world is a combination of a complete specification of the way the world is, $w$, with a complete set of rules for acting and for approving/disapproving of things, $n$. We can represent a factual-normative world as an ordered pair, $\langle w, n \rangle$.
  AFTER:  The latest in anti-realist technology, Allan Gibbard's 'factual-normative worlds' are abstract objects designed to help explain the meanings both of simple evaluative statements and of complex sentences with evaluative components. A factual-normative world is a combination of a complete specification of the way the world is, $w$, with a complete set of rules for acting and for approving/disapproving of things, $n$. We can represent a factual-normative world as an ordered pair, $\langle w, n \rangle$.
