Line 201:
  BEFORE: From these worlds and these norm-sets, four factual-normative worlds can be formed: $\langle w_1, n_1 \rangle$, $\langle w_1, n_2 \rangle$, $\langle w_2, n_1 \rangle$, and $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$. The statement 'If stealing the candy was wrong, then God will punish you' holds in $\langle w_1, n_1 \rangle$, $\langle w_1, n_2 \rangle$, and $\langle w_2, n_1 \rangle$; it does not hold in $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$. So $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$ is the only one of the four worlds that the speaker's statement rules out. Gibbard's view can be applied equally well to more complex statements and more general statements about wrongful actions (for instance, 'If it is wrong to do something, then it is wrong to get your little brother to do it').
  AFTER:  From these worlds and these norm-sets, four factual-normative worlds can be formed: $\langle w_1, n_1 \rangle$, $\langle w_1, n_2 \rangle$, $\langle w_2, n_1 \rangle$, and $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$. The statement 'If stealing the candy was wrong, then God will punish you' holds in $\langle w_1, n_1 \rangle$, $\langle w_1, n_2 \rangle$, and $\langle w_2, n_1 \rangle$; it does not hold in $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$. So $\langle w_2, n_2 \rangle$ is the only one of the four worlds that the speaker's statement rules out. Gibbard's view can be applied equally well to more complex statements and more general statements about wrongful actions (for instance, 'If it is wrong to do something, then it is wrong to get your little brother to do it').
